WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.170
Here's the thing, guys: the handheld gaming PC market is booming,

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:07.190
and the Steam Deck is the main driver of that growth.

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:11.490
Not that these PCs didn't exist before, but when everyone saw the Steam Deck they said:

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:14.590
"I want one. I don't know why, but I want one."

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:18.810
After Valve, several companies followed the same path and built devices like it,

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:21.310
and the most successful one was Asus.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:26.310
This device here is theirs: the ROG Ally X, or "Rog" Ally X, however you want to say it.

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:31.410
Please don't correct me in the comments. Someone will anyway.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:32.970
Still, it's basically a Steam Deck Pro.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:37.230
Its processor is a Ryzen Z1 Extreme, more powerful than the Steam Deck's, and it has more RAM,

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:41.150
and a screen that isn't OLED like the Steam Deck OLED,

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:46.750
but it's a Full HD LCD at 120 Hz with variable refresh rate support.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:50.410
On top of all that, the Ally has an 80 Wh battery,

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:53.930
versus 50 on the Steam Deck OLED and 40 on the Steam Deck LCD.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:56.510
That's laptop-sized. Way bigger.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:59.490
The engineering to fit that in here is impressive.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:05.930
It's practically perfect, except for one thing.

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:08.290
It has Windows. It ships with Windows.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.910
Let me explain something for those who aren't up to date on this.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:13.790
The Steam Deck runs an operating system called SteamOS, which is Linux-based,

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:16.790
but it runs Windows games.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:20.170
How does it run Windows games if Linux is a different system?

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.670
It uses a compatibility layer called Proton,

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:25.930
which translates a Windows game's instructions for Linux.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:29.070
Do all games run perfectly? No. But most of them do.

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:34.590
"So wait, if not every game runs properly on it,

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:37.270
wouldn't it be better to use Windows,

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:41.830
since on Windows every Windows game runs with no problem?

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:47.710
Better to have all the games than almost all the games, right? Right?"

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.150
No. Wrong.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.330
That's what we thought when the Steam Deck came out.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:57.090
But then we used Windows 11 on these tiny-screen devices,

00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:03.750
touchscreen only, no keyboard and mouse, and the experience is infuriating.

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:07.270
So much so that I have several devices more powerful than the Steam Deck,

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:09.690
but I always end up going back to the Steam Deck, because I don't want to deal with Windows.

00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:14.910
"But not every game works on the Steam Deck, you're going to..."

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:19.110
Doesn't matter. Doesn't matter. Let me explain,

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:25.850
starting with the classic Windows problems that show up the first time you boot the device.

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:27.810
It will try to push a Microsoft Office install on you,

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:32.430
on every major Windows update, to sell you a subscription later.

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:37.100
Why? Because Microsoft Office is Microsoft's second biggest source of revenue.

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:39.910
If you get distracted and click the wrong button once, it installs Microsoft Office.

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:44.550
Is it a bad suite of apps? No, it's actually very good.

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:51.830
Do I need it on a small handheld built to play video games? Absolutely not.

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:55.610
And the bigger incompetence: Microsoft could be promoting Game Pass

00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:57.590
with the same effort they put behind Microsoft Office,

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:00.990
which is also a Microsoft subscription service, for games,

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:04.750
and would make much more sense on a device like this.

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:10.050
But Microsoft is in no hurry, right? The company's other divisions make billions.

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:12.070
They're swimming in money, let's be serious.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:16.070
But so far I've only talked about setup and operating system updates,

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:20.270
because updating a handheld like this is a problem in itself.

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:26.670
Say you're playing a Steam game on it. You already have to keep the game updated through the Steam app.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:28.970
Count with me: that's number one.

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:34.150
But you also need to open Windows Update in the settings to keep Windows updated.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:35.450
That's number two.

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:40.250
Then you have to open Armoury Crate to update some drivers

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:45.310
and the gaming interface Asus runs on top of Windows to make the experience less painful.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:48.150
Thanks, Asus. But that's three.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:53.690
You still have to update things through the MyAsus app, standard on every Asus PC,

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:56.670
to download drivers like Bluetooth and audio. That's four.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:01.370
One of those two Asus apps also handles the AMD GPU update,

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:07.230
but you'll want the AMD app too, Adrenalin, to control some GPU functions.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:13.770
So that's one more place to update one more thing on your device. Five. Five.

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:17.110
Meanwhile, how does it work on the Steam Deck?

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:24.630
You have your game update, as usual, and one update in the settings menu for everything else.

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:27.930
All of it in a single app.

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:32.430
Poor Asus puts a lot, a lot of effort into improving the Windows experience.

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:37.930
That's why it ships with Armoury Crate and Command Center to manage so many aspects of the device.

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:42.290
But nothing can fix Windows' problems. Microsoft has to do that.

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:47.330
Like, on the Steam Deck those extra functions for controlling power draw and frame rate

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:49.230
are all mapped to a controller. On Windows? No.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:56.670
On the Steam Deck you can suspend the game and come back later, sure it will still be running.

00:04:56.720 --> 00:04:57.830
On Windows? No.

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:02.310
And there's a lot more. We could be here all day talking about this, but you get it.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:08.030
Not just you: Microsoft too. So much so that Microsoft's VP of Next Generation

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:12.830
recently said they're working on a version of Windows specifically for these handhelds.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:17.010
They're working on it, which is good. Is it available? No.

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:22.930
So what we can do is install SteamOS on the ROG Ally.

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:26.650
Then we get the best of both worlds: the battery, the 120 Hz screen with variable refresh rate,

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:34.250
the more powerful processor, and no Windows. No Windows.

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:40.110
But here's the catch. Valve, who makes the Steam Deck,

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:45.470
promised to make SteamOS available for anyone to download and install wherever they want.

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:48.410
Promised? Yes. Delivered? Not yet.

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:52.570
But there's a big "but" here, because SteamOS is Linux.

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:55.090
Linux is free and open source.

00:05:55.140 --> 00:06:00.130
If Valve won't give you SteamOS, you can go and make it yourself. Anyone can.

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:07.570
You just need a lot of programming knowledge, a lot of Linux knowledge, and a lot of free time.

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:13.950
Luckily for us, someone did it. More specifically, a very dedicated group of people.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:16.190
They maintain a project called Bazzite, a Linux distribution

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:23.470
where they replicated how SteamOS works, bit by bit,

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:27.570
except where it wasn't possible, because different devices have different hardware.

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:29.590
But it's basically the same thing.

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:36.710
So you can install SteamOS wherever you want. They make it available to download for free.

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:40.550
For free. You can turn your device into a Steam Deck.

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:44.650
So, ladies and gentlemen, now we're going to install Bazzite, basically SteamOS,

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:49.790
on the ROG Ally X and see the results. Let's go.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:52.850
First I had to download Bazzite from their site:

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:59.730
click Download, choose the device, ROG Ally X, and the KDE desktop environment to match SteamOS.

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:03.350
Then I downloaded an app, Balena Etcher, to make an install USB stick

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:05.450
with the Bazzite ISO I had just downloaded.

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:08.690
In Balena Etcher I selected the ISO and the USB stick, hit Flash, and done:

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:11.150
I had a Bazzite install stick.

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:16.450
Then on the ROG Ally X I went into the security settings and decrypted its SSD.

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:21.730
Next I downloaded a program called Paragon Partition Manager to split my SSD in two,

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:25.770
and gave 512 GB to the partition where I'd install Bazzite.

00:07:25.820 --> 00:07:30.170
Then I plugged a USB hub with keyboard, mouse and my Bazzite stick into the Ally X,

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:34.070
and restarted it holding volume down to get into the BIOS.

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:38.230
In the BIOS I went to boot priority and dragged the USB stick to the top,

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:40.910
then clicked the top-left button and saved.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:44.470
When it booted from the Bazzite stick, I told it to install,

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:47.630
selected the language, then the install location.

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:50.670
Since the SSD was already partitioned, it was just a matter of clicking Done,

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:54.430
then I set a username and password and continued the installation.

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:57.250
After installing, it reboots into the Secure Boot menu.

00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:04.470
I clicked Enroll, OK, Continue, Yes, and typed the password "universalblue", from the Bazzite folks.

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:10.650
Then it rebooted and opened straight into the Steam login screen. It's installed.

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:13.610
But seriously, guys, when I started working on this video I thought:

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:17.170
this will be a fun experiment, a cool thing to test and see how it works.

00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:22.670
But let me tell you something. This is sensational. Sensational.

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:25.750
The ROG Ally X with Bazzite is basically a Steam Deck Pro.

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:27.630
It's everything we asked for. It works wonderfully.

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:32.410
You get the 120 Hz screen with variable refresh rate, the bigger battery,

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:35.670
and the much more powerful processor.

00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:41.530
It has all the Steam Deck functions. I can click here and open that menu to control performance,

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:47.970
I can control the screen refresh rate, turn on variable refresh rate,

00:08:48.020 --> 00:08:50.690
control the screen scaling,

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:58.650
and even the performance overlay. I'll turn it on and set it to the biggest, most detailed level.

00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:02.690
There are the numbers to track the game's performance, the same way I have on the Steam Deck.

00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:05.490
The only thing actually missing is the power control.

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:11.050
But what you can do is double-click on it, and it gives you this control here,

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:14.570
where you can change how much power the device draws.

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:19.970
And if your game freezes you can use this menu to close it too. Confirm. Done.

00:09:20.020 --> 00:09:28.810
And everything really works. You have access to your library, all there, just like on the Steam Deck.

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:32.730
And if you need to update your system or change anything,

00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:37.300
you go to Settings, here under System, and check for updates.

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:43.110
No update. All good.

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:48.410
And just like on the Steam Deck, you can use an external controller to pull up these menus.

00:09:48.460 --> 00:09:50.850
The button mapping is even the same.

00:09:50.900 --> 00:09:53.750
And you can play tons of games on it. I tested several,

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:58.570
even games without official support, that aren't "Deck Verified", so to speak.

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:03.790
On top of that, you can play lots of older, lighter games at 120 Hz,

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:06.270
because that's the screen's maximum refresh rate.

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:10.010
Obviously there are unsupported games, not Deck Verified, that will give you trouble,

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:13.770
but they give you trouble on the Steam Deck too.

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:18.210
But the best part, ladies and gentlemen: for games that don't work on Steam Deck or Bazzite,

00:10:18.260 --> 00:10:21.480
you can do this.

00:10:21.530 --> 00:10:32.270
Power, restart, confirm, and hold volume up while it reboots.

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:37.610
That takes you to the dual boot screen, and you can pick Windows,

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:41.690
because I kept Windows on the machine. I have both operating systems.

00:10:41.740 --> 00:10:47.210
Here we have, ladies and gentlemen, Windows 11 running. All functions intact.

00:10:47.260 --> 00:10:53.600
You can use this for unsupported games, but also for Game Pass games.

00:10:56.520 --> 00:10:59.080
Asus likes its little noises, you know.

00:10:59.130 --> 00:11:07.100
But I'll confess something: I gave Bazzite 512 GB and the rest of the SSD to Windows.

00:11:07.150 --> 00:11:09.480
If I had to do it again, I'd leave much less space for Windows,

00:11:09.530 --> 00:11:12.520
because I don't see myself going back to Windows for anything.

00:11:12.570 --> 00:11:19.440
At most I'd leave room for one game that happens not to work on Bazzite.

00:11:19.490 --> 00:11:24.820
But at least one thing saves you here: the SD card.

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:29.080
The Steam games you install on it are available to both Windows and Bazzite.

00:11:29.130 --> 00:11:36.400
So with one install you can open the game on both. You can even use that to compare performance, if you want.

00:11:36.450 --> 00:11:40.780
Now back to Bazzite, because I have two last things to show you.

00:11:40.830 --> 00:11:44.800
First, SteamOS screen recording works.

00:11:44.850 --> 00:11:48.280
If I look here under Media, I've already recorded some files.

00:11:48.330 --> 00:11:53.120
You can record internally, straight from the overlay menu over the game,

00:11:53.170 --> 00:11:57.000
and you get your gameplay recorded at up to 1080p.

00:11:57.050 --> 00:12:03.620
And finally, the sensational thing, the complete game changer for this kind of device: this.

00:12:03.670 --> 00:12:08.680
You put it in standby. It goes to standby.

00:12:08.730 --> 00:12:14.800
You come back, no cuts, and it resumes exactly where you were.

00:12:14.850 --> 00:12:20.560
What's so hard, Windows? Why can't a billion-dollar company like Microsoft,

00:12:20.610 --> 00:12:23.360
trillion-dollar, I guess, they must be past a trillion,

00:12:23.410 --> 00:12:27.000
make this work reliably?

00:12:27.050 --> 00:12:30.180
Seriously, let me show you something. This is Tembo, if I'm not mistaken.

00:12:30.230 --> 00:12:33.520
It's not Deck Verified, no "official support".

00:12:33.570 --> 00:12:40.080
I'm playing the game. I pause, click to suspend, it suspends.

00:12:40.130 --> 00:12:44.390
Then I come back here, when I want to play, and click again.

00:12:47.140 --> 00:12:53.230
It's exactly where I was. I can unpause and keep playing. Magic, isn't it?

00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:57.210
But now, guys, the negative part.

00:12:57.260 --> 00:13:00.850
In my whole experience with Bazzite on the ROG Ally X I had two more-or-less serious problems.

00:13:00.900 --> 00:13:06.110
First, pairing Bluetooth controllers took more work than I'd like.

00:13:06.160 --> 00:13:12.230
I don't know what happens: it lists a bunch of devices, I select the controller, and it doesn't always connect.

00:13:12.280 --> 00:13:16.710
But once I managed to pair a controller, it worked with no problem.

00:13:16.760 --> 00:13:21.130
And one thing I couldn't do is use the shortcut for the on-screen keyboard,

00:13:21.180 --> 00:13:25.130
which is pressing the Steam button and X together.

00:13:25.180 --> 00:13:29.270
It works on an external controller, but not on the ROG Ally itself.

00:13:29.320 --> 00:13:35.130
From what I read online, there's a hardware limitation on that ROG Ally button

00:13:35.180 --> 00:13:40.170
when combined with another button on the device, so you can't trigger both together.

00:13:40.220 --> 00:13:45.690
And I know it's a ROG Ally limitation, not a Bazzite one, because it works with an external controller.

00:13:45.740 --> 00:13:51.030
Apparently there's a way around it. Someone already made a hack you can download to make it work.

00:13:51.080 --> 00:13:57.290
But the point of this video was to see how easy it is to install Bazzite on the ROG Ally X

00:13:57.340 --> 00:14:04.390
and use it without configuring much, that plug-and-play experience. So I didn't do it.

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:08.890
If you're looking to do something like this, keep that limitation in mind.

00:14:08.940 --> 00:14:14.470
Some games ask for a keyboard, I don't know why, but you need one to get through a menu.

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:19.130
And the only Deck Verified game that gave me trouble was Spider-Man 2.

00:14:19.180 --> 00:14:24.910
It froze completely while I was playing and I had to restart the device.

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:30.710
But I think that's the game's problem, not the system's, because it has a reputation for terrible optimization.

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:36.850
Sony is releasing patches, but the PC launch of that game was definitely not smooth.

00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:44.610
But given everything I've seen so far, this was the best experience I've had with these handheld gaming PCs.

00:14:44.660 --> 00:14:46.910
Seriously, guys, I don't think I can overstate it.

00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:52.730
I see no reason to go back to the Steam Deck or use any experience that isn't this, or very close to it.

00:14:52.780 --> 00:14:58.450
And Valve knows it. Recently there were leaks that they're already preparing

00:14:58.500 --> 00:15:04.010
the generic version of SteamOS for you to install on your device.

00:15:04.060 --> 00:15:06.090
You won't even need to go through Bazzite.

00:15:06.140 --> 00:15:12.490
But Bazzite itself is already extremely impressive, because this is community work.

00:15:12.540 --> 00:15:16.650
It's people volunteering their time to build this kind of system for you to install on your device.

00:15:16.700 --> 00:15:20.590
And here you don't have the problems you have on Windows 11,

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:24.910
an operating system from a multi-billion-dollar company. I don't get it.

00:15:24.960 --> 00:15:31.270
And a device like the ROG Ally or Ally X is great for this kind of install,

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:34.890
because it's so popular you'll most likely find support for everything on it.

00:15:34.940 --> 00:15:42.690
The Bazzite folks even make a specific build for it that works out of the box, nothing to install on top.

00:15:42.740 --> 00:15:46.810
An advantage you wouldn't have trying to install Bazzite on more niche devices,

00:15:46.860 --> 00:15:50.570
like the AYANEO I reviewed here on the channel before.

00:15:50.620 --> 00:15:54.910
To get it working on a device like that you'd probably have a lot more work and a lot more to install on top.

00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:58.910
Anyway, if you have a device like this and you're thinking of trying Bazzite,

00:15:58.960 --> 00:16:03.110
I'll put links to some guides in the description.

00:16:03.160 --> 00:16:08.800
And don't forget to leave a like and subscribe. I think I'll see you in the next one. Bye!
